Push event selection

⚠ IMPORTANT

If your Jenkins is local, you MUST run ngrok to expose port 8080.

Command
ngrok http 8080


Copy the Forwarding URL and use it in the GitHub webhook.

✅ STEP 2: Configure Jenkins to Receive Webhook
Install Plugin
Manage Jenkins → Plugins → GitHub Integration Plugin

Enable webhook trigger inside your Jenkins job

For a Freestyle Project:

Build Triggers → GitHub hook trigger for GITScm polling


For a Pipeline Job:

Inside Jenkinsfile add:

pipeline {
    agent any
    triggers {
        githubPush()
    }
    stages {
        stage('Build') {
            steps {
                echo "Code build triggered from GitHub webhook!"
            }
        }
    }
}

✅ STEP 3: Push a Code Change

Modify any file:

echo "// test change" >> test.java
git add .
git commit -m "webhook test"
git push origin main

✅ STEP 4: Jenkins Auto-Triggered Build

When you push → GitHub sends webhook → Jenkins automatically starts a build.

On Jenkins Dashboard:

You will see:

Started by GitHub push by <username>
Running on Jenkins in /var/lib/jenkins/workspace/<project>
[Pipeline] Start
...
[Pipeline] echo
Code build triggered from GitHub webhook!
[Pipeline] End
Finished: SUCCESS

